var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#Lexbor.jl","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"This package provides a Julia interface to the lexbor HTML parsing library. Lexbor.jl integrates with AbstractTrees.jl to provide an interface for traversing the HTML tree.\n\nThe exposed parts of the library are HTML parsing, DOM querying, node navigation, attribute access, and HTML serialization.","title":"Lexbor.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Usage","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"The package exports it's public interface, but prefer using qualified identifiers rather than using.\n\nimport Lexbor","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"#Parsing-HTML","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Create a new DOM object using the Document constructor.\n\ndoc = Lexbor.Document(\"<div class='callout'><a href='#'>Link</a></div>\")\n\nOr you can parse a file with Base.open:\n\ndoc = open(Lexbor.Document, \"file.html\")","title":"Parsing HTML"},{"category":"section","location":"#Querying-documents","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Use query to search for nodes within the document that match the provided CSS selector.\n\nlinks = Lexbor.query(doc, \"a\")\n\ncallouts = Lexbor.query(doc, \"div.callout\")\n\nquery also supports passing a function as the first argument that will be called on each matching Node that is found. Using this method avoids allocating a vector and iterating over the results twice if they don't need to be stored.\n\nLexbor.query(doc, \"a\") do link\n    @show Lexbor.attributes(link)\nend","title":"Querying documents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Iteration","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"You can use any AbstractTrees iterators to traverse the document contents.\n\nimport AbstractTrees\n\nfor node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(Lexbor.Node(doc))\n    if Lexbor.is_text(node)\n        @show Lexbor.text(node)\n    end\nend\n\nThis uses is_text to check whether the current node is a plain text Node and then displays the text content of it using text. Note that newlines and other whitespace is preserved by lexbor's parsing.\n\nOther predicates and accessors available are:\n\nis_element\nis_comment\ntag\ncomment","title":"Iteration"},{"category":"section","location":"#Matching-Nodes","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Matcher allows for testing a Node to determine whether it matches the given CSS selector.\n\nmatcher = Lexbor.Matcher(\"div.callout\")\nfor node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(Lexbor.Node(doc))\n    if matcher(node)\n        @show node\n    end\nend\n\nAs with query you can pass a function as the first argument to a Matcher object in which case it will get called when the Node matches and will return nothing instead of true/false.\n\nfor node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(Lexbor.Node(doc))\n    matcher(node) do matched\n        @show matched\n    end\nend","title":"Matching Nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"#Navigating-nodes","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Move around the tree relative to a given Node. Each accessor returns a Node or nothing when there is no such node.\n\ndiv = only(Lexbor.query(doc, \"div.callout\"))\nlink = only(Lexbor.query(doc, \"a\"))\nLexbor.parentnode(link)\nLexbor.nextsibling(link)\nLexbor.prevsibling(link)\nLexbor.lastchild(div)\n\nThe accessors are parentnode, nextsibling, prevsibling, and lastchild.","title":"Navigating nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"#Attribute-access","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Read a single attribute of an element with attribute. It returns the value as a String, or nothing when the attribute is absent or present but valueless.\n\nLexbor.attribute(link, \"href\")\nLexbor.attribute(link, \"target\")\n\nhasattribute tells an absent attribute apart from a valueless one.\n\nLexbor.hasattribute(link, \"href\")\nLexbor.hasattribute(link, \"target\")\n\nUse attributes to read every attribute of an element as a Dict.\n\nLexbor.attributes(link)","title":"Attribute access"},{"category":"section","location":"#Serializing-documents","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Serialize a Node or Document back to HTML. outerhtml includes the node itself; innerhtml emits its descendants only.\n\nLexbor.outerhtml(div)\nLexbor.innerhtml(div)\nLexbor.outerhtml(doc)","title":"Serializing documents"},{"category":"section","location":"#Document-accessors","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Reach the standard parts of a parsed document with title, head, and body.\n\npage = Lexbor.Document(\"<html><head><title>Example</title></head><body><p>Hi</p></body></html>\")\nLexbor.title(page)\nLexbor.head(page)\nLexbor.body(page)","title":"Document accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"#API","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Document","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Document(html_str)\nopen(Document, html_file_path)\n\nParse HTML into an in-memory tree representing the DOM. To parse an HTML file use Base.open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Document"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Matcher","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Matcher(selector; first = false)\n\nCreate a new Matcher object that can be used to test whether a Node matches the selector or not. Matcher objects are callable and can be used as follows:\n\nfunction find_first_node(root, selector)\n    # Create the `Matcher` object once, then reuse in the loop.\n    matcher = Matcher(selector)\n    for node in AbstractTrees.PreOrderDFS(root)\n        if matcher(node)\n            return node\n        end\n    end\n    return nothing\nend\n\nThe matcher object has two callable methods. The first, shown above, returns true or false depending on whether the selector matches. The other method takes a first argument function ::Node -> Nothing that is called when the selector matches.\n\nThe keyword argument first::Bool has the same behaviour as the first keyword provided by query. See that function's documentation for details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Matcher"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Node","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Node(document::Document)\n\nAn iterable object representing a particular node within an HTML Document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Node"},{"category":"type","location":"#Lexbor.Tree","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"Tree(document)\nTree(node)\n\nA display type to help visualize DOM structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.Tree"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.attribute-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"attribute(node::Node, name) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the value of the name attribute of an element node, or nothing.\n\nnothing is returned both when the attribute is absent and when it is present but valueless (e.g. v-slot:avatar in <template v-slot:avatar>), mirroring how attributes represents valueless attributes with a nothing value. Use hasattribute to tell the two cases apart. nothing is also returned when node is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.attribute"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.attributes-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"attributes(node::Node) -> Dict{String,Union{String,Nothing}} | Nothing\n\nReturn a Dict of all the attributes of a node, or nothing when it is not a valid element node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.attributes"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.body-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"body(document::Document) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the document's <body> element, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.body"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.comment-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"comment(node) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the comment content of a node, or nothing when the node is not a valid comment node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.comment"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.hasattribute-Tuple{Node, AbstractString}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"hasattribute(node::Node, name) -> Bool\n\nReturn whether an element node has the name attribute, regardless of whether it has a value. Returns false when node is not an element.\n\nThis disambiguates attribute, which returns nothing both for an absent attribute and for a present-but-valueless one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.hasattribute"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.head-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"head(document::Document) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the document's <head> element, or nothing when it has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.head"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.innerhtml-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"innerhtml(node::Node) -> String\ninnerhtml(document::Document) -> String\n\nReturn the HTML serialization of the node's descendants only. For a leaf node the returned string is empty; see outerhtml to include the node itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.innerhtml"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.is_comment-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"is_comment(node::Node) -> Bool\n\nIs node a comment Node? E.g. <!-- ... --> syntax.\n\nUse comment to access the String contents of the comment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.is_comment"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.is_element-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"is_element(node::Node) -> Bool\n\nIs the node an HTML element Node? E.g. a <a>, <div>, etc.\n\nUse tag to access the name of the element as a Symbol and use attributes to access the element attributes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.is_element"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.is_text-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"is_text(node::Node) -> Bool\n\nIs the node a plain text string?\n\nUse text to access the String contents of the node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.is_text"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.lastchild-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"lastchild(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the last child of node, or nothing when it has no children.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.lastchild"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.nextsibling-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"nextsibling(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the next sibling of node, or nothing when it is the last child of its parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.nextsibling"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.outerhtml-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"outerhtml(node::Node) -> String\nouterhtml(document::Document) -> String\n\nReturn the HTML serialization of the node and its descendants. For a text node the returned string is the escaped text; see innerhtml for the descendants without the node itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.outerhtml"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.parentnode-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"parentnode(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the parent of node, or nothing when it has no parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.parentnode"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.prevsibling-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"prevsibling(node::Node) -> Node | nothing\n\nReturn the previous sibling of node, or nothing when it is the first child of its parent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.prevsibling"},{"category":"function","location":"#Lexbor.query","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"query(document | node, selector; first = false, root = false) -> Node[]\nquery(f, document | node, selector; first = false, root = false) -> nothing\n\nQuery the document or node for the given CSS selector. When f is provided then call f on each match that is found and return nothing from query. When no f is provided then just return a Vector{Node} containing all matches.\n\nThe first::Bool keyword controls whether to only match the first of a selector list. To quote the upstream documentation:\n\nStop searching after the first match with any of the selectors in the list.By default, the callback will be triggered for each selector list. That is, if your node matches different selector lists, it will be returned multiple times in the callback.For example:HTML: <div id=\"ok\"><span>test</span></div>\nSelectors: div, div[id=\"ok\"], div:has(:not(a))The default behavior will cause three callbacks with the same node (div). Because it will be found by every selector in the list.This option allows you to end the element check after the first match on any of the selectors. That is, the callback will be called only once for example above. This way we get rid of duplicates in the search.\n\nThe root::Bool keyword controls whether to include the root node in the search. To quote the upstream documentation:\n\nIncludes the passed (root) node in the search.By default, the root node does not participate in selector searches, only its children.This behavior is logical, if you have found a node and then you want to search for other nodes in it, you don't need to check it again.But there are cases when it is necessary for root node to participate in the search.  That's what this option is for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.query"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.tag-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"tag(node) -> Symbol | Nothing\n\nReturn the element tag name, or nothing when it is not an element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.tag"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.text-Tuple{Node}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"text(node) -> String | Nothing\n\nReturn the text content of a node, or nothing when the node is not a valid text node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.text"},{"category":"method","location":"#Lexbor.title-Tuple{Document}","page":"Lexbor.jl","text":"title(document::Document) -> String | nothing\n\nReturn the text of the document's <title> element, or nothing when the document has no <title>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Lexbor.title"}]
}
